WEBVTT

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:06.917
Gemini 3.8 Flash took 17 minutes to code
this world, and half a pixel broke it.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:10.467
But Gemini 4 Argon took 4,
and it's actually playable.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:15.283
Ten challenges, one attempt, and the one
called Flash took 39 minutes longer.

00:00:18.917 --> 00:00:23.217
I'm Kevin, this is Mixiuh Labs,
and the lab opens with a tournament.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:28.233
Gemini 4 Argon versus Gemini 3.8 Flash,
both on High, inside Antigravity.

00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:31.800
Same prompt for both,
one single attempt, no corrections.

00:00:31.833 --> 00:00:36.533
And both shipped each challenge
as a single HTML file, no images.

00:00:36.567 --> 00:00:39.950
And I timed every generation,
rounded to the minute.

00:00:39.983 --> 00:00:43.350
And from now on, house rule:
here, we don't guess, we test.

00:00:43.383 --> 00:00:48.283
Five worlds, ten levels, and I keep score,
judging quality and functionality.

00:00:48.317 --> 00:00:53.033
Coming up in level 6: the exact line
where half a pixel breaks a world.

00:00:53.417 --> 00:00:55.250
World 1, 3D graphics:

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:58.900
a solar system where the Moon
inherits the Earth's motion.

00:00:58.933 --> 00:01:03.017
And the first surprise: Flash finishes
sooner, seven minutes versus eleven.

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:07.467
Its Earth looks good, with clouds
and city lights on the night side.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.383
But up close, its Sun is a white disc,
as bright as a bathroom bulb.

00:01:12.417 --> 00:01:17.450
In Argon, I dim the glow, and the Sun's
surface appears, with cells and spots.

00:01:17.483 --> 00:01:22.283
Both have eclipses, but in my recording,
the Flash one is just text.

00:01:22.317 --> 00:01:26.533
In Argon, Earth's shadow paints
the Moon red: a blood moon.

00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:30.883
And it draws its family tree on screen:
Sun, then Earth, then Moon.

00:01:30.917 --> 00:01:33.850
Flash won the race,
but Argon won the level,

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:37.433
with better graphics and a better-designed
background: one to zero.

00:01:37.817 --> 00:01:43.000
Level 2: a DNA helix made of particles,
and Flash boasts 18,480.

00:01:43.033 --> 00:01:46.000
That number is hardcoded
and never changes,

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.283
and its code actually generates 17,840.

00:01:49.317 --> 00:01:53.467
That said, its top-down view looks
like a galaxy, and it's gorgeous.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:58.483
Argon starts so bright the helix
looks blown out, until I dim it.

00:01:58.517 --> 00:02:03.750
Here are its 24,500 particles,
with a counter that's actually real.

00:02:03.783 --> 00:02:06.633
In Flash, the shockwave
barely moves the helix.

00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:11.200
Argon's helix explodes into a white cloud
and rebuilds itself, like a spring.

00:02:11.233 --> 00:02:15.850
And Flash doesn't have the density
and shape controls the prompt asked for.

00:02:15.883 --> 00:02:21.250
Argon wins on quality and on time,
5 minutes versus 7: two to zero.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:26.150
So far it's a style fight,
but in level 4, one ships in 2 minutes,

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:27.433
and look what it shipped.

00:02:28.067 --> 00:02:33.200
World 2, procedural and physics:
a flock where your mouse is the predator.

00:02:33.233 --> 00:02:36.167
And here, honestly, it was dead even.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:40.150
Flash finished in 3 minutes,
added sound I never asked for,

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:42.200
and its flock is incredible.

00:02:42.233 --> 00:02:44.467
Argon took 5 and went
the scientific route,

00:02:44.467 --> 00:02:46.517
with a shortcut for every debug layer.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:51.033
Both split space into a grid,
but I only turned that view on in Argon.

00:02:51.067 --> 00:02:54.067
And another key releases
up to three predators with their own

00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:55.633
AI: the leviathans.

00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:57.183
My note: both deliver,

00:02:57.183 --> 00:03:00.567
but the customization
and shortcuts give Argon the win.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.300
A win on points, not a knockout:

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:05.267
three to zero, though Flash
still leads the overall clock.

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:08.517
Level 4, dungeons, and here
Flash worked for two minutes,

00:03:08.517 --> 00:03:10.783
decided it was done, and handed me this.

00:03:10.817 --> 00:03:13.633
At first it looks fine,
but in step-by-step mode,

00:03:13.633 --> 00:03:15.550
the path appears before the rooms.

00:03:15.583 --> 00:03:18.700
Then a tangle of purple lines
cuts across the whole map.

00:03:18.733 --> 00:03:21.983
In explorer mode, there
are no enemies, no traps, no life.

00:03:21.983 --> 00:03:25.750
Argon took 12 minutes, its slowest
delivery, and built a little RPG.

00:03:25.783 --> 00:03:29.133
A trap takes 12 HP, a chest heals 10,

00:03:29.133 --> 00:03:32.333
and finally I beat
the Guardian of the Abyss.

00:03:32.367 --> 00:03:37.567
I reach the stairs and get a new level,
but in Flash, the exit does nothing.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.617
Flash was six times faster, sure,
but only at calling it done.

00:03:44.217 --> 00:03:47.917
Four to zero, but Flash leads
the clock by 14 minutes,

00:03:47.917 --> 00:03:51.250
a cushion that lasts until level 6.

00:03:51.633 --> 00:03:55.200
Here's what happens when lava
hits a garden in Argon's sandbox.

00:03:55.233 --> 00:04:00.600
Level 5, a pixel sandbox, and the basic
test is that water settles flat.

00:04:00.633 --> 00:04:04.500
In Flash, water pushed
by sand stays stuck in a dome.

00:04:04.533 --> 00:04:08.033
In Argon, the wave spreads out
and levels like real water.

00:04:08.067 --> 00:04:12.450
Before you say it: both have 12 elements,
and Flash's gunpowder looks great.

00:04:12.483 --> 00:04:16.167
But only Argon's plant
drinks the water to grow.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:21.183
If I burn it, the shelf gives way,
and the hourglass runs again.

00:04:24.617 --> 00:04:29.233
For me, Argon lapped it twice,
and its chemistry is ten times better.

00:04:29.267 --> 00:04:31.350
In under half the time, five to zero,

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:34.767
and Flash's cushion drops
to 9 minutes before level 6.

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:38.283
World 3, pixel art and retro,
and here comes the boss:

00:04:38.283 --> 00:04:41.233
a Wolfenstein-style engine from 1992.

00:04:41.267 --> 00:04:44.750
One ray per screen column fakes
walls in three dimensions.

00:04:44.783 --> 00:04:48.717
Flash took 17 minutes,
and its first frame looks perfect.

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:51.567
One step, and the world breaks.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:54.200
The walls break into strips,
and in places,

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:55.933
the world almost disappears.

00:04:55.967 --> 00:05:00.050
And its counter happily
reads 120 frames per second.

00:05:00.083 --> 00:05:01.700
The culprit is half a pixel:

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:04.533
Flash divides each wall's
height by two and doesn't round.

00:05:04.567 --> 00:05:06.983
With odd heights you get a point five,

00:05:06.983 --> 00:05:10.067
and JavaScript silently
skips that whole column.

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:14.100
Half a pixel, 17 minutes, and game over.

00:05:14.133 --> 00:05:19.800
Argon used a bit shift that always gives
an integer, and solved it in 4 minutes.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:26.150
And look: sliding doors, orbs,
drones, and a plasma cannon.

00:05:26.183 --> 00:05:27.800
There's no defending Flash here:

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:32.617
six to zero, and Argon pulls ahead
on the clock too, 41 minutes versus 45.

00:05:33.467 --> 00:05:36.550
Twelve different ships in twelve seconds,
and none drawn by hand.

00:05:36.583 --> 00:05:40.783
Level 7: 8-bit pixel art
ships that you can also fly.

00:05:40.817 --> 00:05:44.283
In Flash, I hit mutate about 55 times,

00:05:44.283 --> 00:05:47.900
and nearly all spit out
the same red-and-gold totem.

00:05:47.933 --> 00:05:51.033
To be fair, partly my fault:
I never changed the palette.

00:05:51.067 --> 00:05:55.317
Something I liked in Flash: it shows
each ship being built, layer by layer.

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:59.250
In the test flight, Flash's targets
die in an almost invisible puff.

00:05:59.283 --> 00:06:03.267
In Argon, rocks and drones burst
into pixels and give points,

00:06:03.267 --> 00:06:04.317
like an arcade.

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:08.667
Flash took 13 minutes and Argon
only 4, and though nothing broke,

00:06:08.667 --> 00:06:11.367
Argon wins again: seven to zero.

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:15.433
Three levels left,
and in one of them Flash takes 22 minutes.

00:06:15.467 --> 00:06:17.317
World 4, CSS magic:

00:06:17.317 --> 00:06:22.067
a luxury watch where JavaScript only sets
the time and never moves the hands.

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:24.250
Both did it by the book:

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:27.950
three themes, three second-hand
rhythms, and a tourbillon.

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:32.283
That said, Flash's transitions
are smoother: point to Flash.

00:06:32.317 --> 00:06:35.083
But Flash's watch looks like an app icon,

00:06:35.083 --> 00:06:37.383
and Argon's looks like
a display-case watch.

00:06:37.417 --> 00:06:40.683
And its Night Luxury mode,
honestly, lives up to the name.

00:06:40.717 --> 00:06:42.350
And in only 2 minutes,

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:45.717
the fastest delivery
that actually worked: eight to zero.

00:06:46.667 --> 00:06:48.583
World 5, pure math:

00:06:48.583 --> 00:06:52.283
a fractal tree that sways
in a wind of mathematical noise.

00:06:52.317 --> 00:06:55.883
Argon delivered it in 4 minutes,
and Flash in 22.

00:06:57.917 --> 00:07:01.783
That's 18 minutes apart,
the biggest gap in the experiment.

00:07:01.817 --> 00:07:07.133
And Flash's cherry tree looks pretty,
bending in the wind and shedding petals.

00:07:07.167 --> 00:07:11.717
But its weeping willow is the same tree
painted green, and it doesn't weep at all.

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:16.050
In Argon, the tree rebuilds itself and
the branches really hang like curtains.

00:07:19.083 --> 00:07:22.217
So nobody calls me a fanboy:
with over 20,000 flower clusters,

00:07:22.217 --> 00:07:25.867
even Argon drops
to about 45 frames per second.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:30.367
Still, like I wrote in my notes, Argon
is an efficiency and quality machine.

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:35.167
Better graphics and more control in under
a fifth of the time: nine to zero.

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:38.483
Final level, a digital spirograph,

00:07:38.483 --> 00:07:40.617
and here Flash botches
some middle-school math.

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:43.350
Both draw the same mandala from scratch.

00:07:43.383 --> 00:07:46.067
Flash traces a smaller,
thin-lined golden rose,

00:07:46.067 --> 00:07:49.400
and Argon traces a huge
mandala with acrylic gears.

00:07:49.433 --> 00:07:52.300
The trick is knowing
when the figure closes:

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:55.433
the greatest common divisor,
finally good for something.

00:07:55.467 --> 00:07:59.950
But with a third gear, Flash does the math
as if that gear didn't exist.

00:07:59.983 --> 00:08:03.433
Its pen speed meter reads
zero for the entire recording.

00:08:03.433 --> 00:08:07.950
Argon does the exact math, and its final
rosette looks like banknote engraving.

00:08:07.983 --> 00:08:12.967
In my recording, it even calculated
a closure at 17,310 radians.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:18.967
Both delivered, but Argon let me customize
far more, and did it 2.5 times faster.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:22.217
Final level, test passed,
and the scoreboard is perfect.

00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:28.150
And it's ten to zero: Gemini 4 Argon
wins on quality and functionality.

00:08:30.917 --> 00:08:34.083
Argon worked in all ten,
and Flash broke in two of them.

00:08:34.117 --> 00:08:38.617
On time, Argon totaled
55 minutes versus 94,

00:08:38.617 --> 00:08:41.967
and 8 of its 10 deliveries
took 5 minutes or less.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:47.533
That said, that score is about quality,
not speed: Flash won three races.

00:08:47.917 --> 00:08:50.433
To me, the fast model
isn't the first to answer,

00:08:50.433 --> 00:08:52.517
it's the one that works on the first try.

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:57.000
Because every bug it leaves behind
is your time spent fixing it,

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:59.050
and no stopwatch counts that.

00:08:59.083 --> 00:09:01.250
Mind you, Flash isn't bad at all:

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:04.067
its flock, clock,
and spirograph turned out well.

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:08.950
In my opinion, Argon is a real
upgrade over previous Gemini models.

00:09:08.983 --> 00:09:10.583
The code's in the description,

00:09:10.583 --> 00:09:13.433
so don't take my word for it:
test it yourself.

00:09:13.467 --> 00:09:17.267
Subscribe, and tell me which models
should go into the lab next.

00:09:17.650 --> 00:09:21.483
Experiment complete: see you
in the next one, here on Mixiuh Labs.
